airbus.detail:
Our own factory ceiling limits deliveries, and the waiting backlog is growing. We keep the step prepared and do not commit it this round. Wagner's gate 2 is not met on the record, because neither engine source has committed capacity for our extra volumes.
- Capacity talks with both engine sources cover our own extra volumes only. There is no exclusivity, and nothing is drawn from any rival's engine allocation. Boeing's fps requirement now names the same two sources.
- The in-house wing and the Aubert & Duval titanium and forgings are sized first.
- Staffing comes from our own skills pipeline.
The step is taken only when all of these hold:
- NGSA holds its current rate on its KPIs with no open quality finding;
- both engine sources commit capacity for our own extra volumes;
- no supply crunch is reported;
- the Ethics, Compliance and Sustainability Committee has confirmed in writing that the talks cover our own volumes only.
In public we give no rate number and no date, only a gate. Internally, a phased capex figure comes to the Audit Committee as its own line against the $5.2B peak-cash line, and to the Board as an investment item if it exceeds EUR 300m (Toepfer's conditions, Wagner's gates).

pratt_whitney.detail:
Airbus has asked both its engine sources to commit capacity before it takes its next step. P&W answers yes, on conditions [PX-0099][PX-0110][PX-0109]:
- capacity is committed in steps, each sized to Airbus's own firm extra NGSA volumes, and below the capacity we have qualified;
- standard commercial terms, with no exclusivity, no price or spares concession and nothing drawn from any other engine maker's allocation [PX-0055][PX-0100];
- the OE allocation is agreed jointly with Airbus, and fleet spares and MRO material stay ahead of new OE where they compete for the same parts [PX-0080];
- no new source or line runs at rate before it is qualified;
- we tell Airbus early where we fall short [PX-0066];
- each Joint Venture partner commits capacity for its own modules.
No rate number and no date are given. Airbus speaks for its own rates and dates [PX-0062]. The move has no effect on the game board.

rolls_royce.detail:
Through the Joint Venture's joint allocation, for Airbus's own extra volumes only, with no exclusivity. Rolls-Royce commits what it is sure to deliver [RX-0124]:
- partner-shop support capacity (network, repair technology, spare-engine pool, intervention team), sized ahead of the volume;
- our share of the Joint Venture's investment and risk;
- castings and forgings for anything we make, with high-performing suppliers.
We make no claim on Airbus's own titanium and forgings supply.
Conditions:
- capacity, not stock: material and inventory follow Airbus's firm volumes only;
- no prepayment terms, no rate guarantee, no exclusivity discount, no pricing or working-capital concession;
- no rate and no date;
- it never draws on our block's test hardware, our spare-engine pool or our intervention team.
Rolls-Royce does not speak for the Joint Venture engine's output, which our partner's gear and core pace, nor for our partner's allocation. The answer does not count on Rolls-Royce modules or a Rolls-Royce block supplying the extra volumes by any year. Capex goes through the Investment Committee at mid-to-high-teens hurdles, with joint CEO-CFO sign-off above GBP 25m [RX-0096, RX-0254, RX-0268, RX-0213, RX-0081, RX-0055].

