recommendations.text:
Hold the fps this round and give no fps date in the public statement. Say plainly what has changed:
- the 10-year at Renton is +0.35, about $0.31B better than 2045's value discounted five years;
- demand is above both makers' slots (2,232 against 1,884 in 2050; demand peaks at 2,300 in 2053);
- the case is still $0.65B short of our line.
This is the last decision year, so a hold means no fps on this board. Record candidly that the market leg holds it back, not technology and not the balance sheet. Keep the staged work as the company's standing plan beyond this game: authority to offer first, the 10-year ramp, code 7, and the production system designed with the airplane.

Pope's memo is our production and safety test. It should state:
- the KPI gate;
- that the MAX is holding 57 a month on stable KPIs, agreed with the FAA, with the LEAP-1B allocation confirmed;
- engineering capacity, supplier readiness and two-front strain for any launch;
- the five-step bridge plan if Renton is chosen; at +0.03 the Renton edge is too small to carry the MAX bridge risk without it;
- confirmation that there is no production-certification concurrency.
The public statement should claim no more readiness than her results show.

