KPI gate: the gate fails if, before the orders are sealed, a quality escape, FAA action or supply-chain shortfall is reported. One example is a LEAP-1B allocation short of 1,368 a year. My veto would then bind on any rate order (none is on the menu). I would also fail the readiness of any launch until the line is stable again.

fps: my seat's gates now pass for a lone 10-year. The KPIs are clean, the MAX has held 57 since 2047, there is no H4 overlap, and code 7 engines are ready with in-service data. It also survives the 2% overrun and +3-year test I set in 2045. I would recommend the launch myself if the business case cleared the Board's line: above +$1B in the expected column, no column below -$1B, and Malave's buffer passing. On this grid that needs $0.65B more. If the CEO brings the lone 10-year anyway, operations will not stand in the way, on these conditions:
- new_site, or Renton with the five-step bridge plan;
- code 7, with durability proven before EIS;
- the type certificate before rate, and a supplier gate at each rate break.

What would move me against a lone 10-year:
- supplier data showing the MAX at 57 is thin;
- a shortfall in the Joint Venture's durability evidence;
- a certification plan that needs production-certification concurrency.

787 Re-engine: operations is ready. I would change only if its business case moved above +$1B, and +0.45 is 2045's +0.74 discounted five years. A GEnx durability gap or a new-part supplier shortfall would move me further against it.

Via Embraer: only if a partner brought IP or capacity Boeing lacks, at a cost within $1B of Solo. On this grid it is $2.45B or more short.

