- a reported supply crunch;
- an NGSA or Joint Venture quality finding or engine slip;
- an A350 or A350F quality or supply event;
- a capacity step committed alongside it with the engine sources uncommitted.
A 787 Re-engine on the record before our order would close it under rule 3.

Capacity step: I move from 'keep prepared' to 'take it' only when all of these hold:
- CFM and the Joint Venture both commit capacity for our own extra volumes, with no exclusivity and nothing from any rival's allocation;
- the Joint Venture shows in-service durability and full-life parts;
- the wing, titanium and forgings are sized;
- NGSA holds 100 a month on its KPIs with no open quality finding.

Delay Tactics: nothing moves me while fps is not public, and even with fps public our 1,200-a-year ceiling means no move gains.

My widebody position for the 2050s, if hold: the A350 family and the A350F in production and kept current for customers, and time on wing for the Trent fleet with Rolls-Royce. Any future Re-engine only with an engine committed and its durability proven.

