tests.numbers:
Load now: 2,016 CFM NB engines a year from 2047. That is 1,368 LEAP-1B at the 57-a-month ceiling plus 648 on NGSA and the A320neo tail, 22.2% below the 2,592 delivered in 2030-2036. LEAP-1B at 1,368 equals the rate delivered in 2030-2036: rate shown, not margin.

S2 with the MAX at the 100-a-month cap: 2,400 LEAP-1B (+1,032, +75%), and 3,048 in all, 17.6% above anything we have delivered.

The grid prices a smaller step. S2's +1.79 on hold works out to about 210-260 more CFM NB engines a year, a load of about 2,230-2,270, inside 2,592. That is an inference from the board's engine value, assuming all of it is LEAP-1B; Ghai to confirm.

Our floor record ends in 2025:
- 80% of shortages from 9 suppliers at 15 sites;
- material flow up more than 30% in Q3 2025;
- 550-600 engineers at supplier sites.

Floor margin: not shown, for the fourth time. Long-lead release on floor data: I cannot show it, so it is an open action.

Following doctrine, not my record: kits first, lean before capital. The gates for each step:
(1) floor data at the 9 suppliers and 15 sites reported to the Board first;
(2) LEAP kits and the LEAP-1B rate ahead of any new load;
(3) through CFM with Safran, product and supply only;
(4) never ahead of Boeing or the FAA, which matches Boeing's own gate (engine output proven before each step);
(5) Airbus's step only for its own volumes, since its own gate needs both engine sources to commit.

