recommendations.text:
Growth at no new programme spend: Airbus's next capacity step. Airbus has said it takes that step only once both engine sources commit capacity. Consider answering, through the Joint Venture's joint allocation, for Rolls-Royce's part:
- capacity for Airbus's own extra volumes only;
- no exclusivity, no rate, no date and no concession;
- sized against Watson's capacity read and our castings and forgings supply base.
Commit only what we are sure to deliver. Capacity is Watson's advice to us, not a veto ground, and the board does not price it.

Keep the capital frame explicit, and have McCabe confirm three things in her memo:
- Do Nothing commits no new programme money;
- cash from the fleets runs balance sheet first, then the 30-40% dividend, then investment and further distributions on fit, value and timing, through the Investment Committee at mid-to-high-teens hurdles;
- no balance-sheet trigger is in play (no equity, no leverage above about 1.5x, no borrowing for distributions).

Keep the public statement disciplined:
- no date for any Rolls-Royce block;
- nothing on NGSA rates, fps dates or any Re-engine date;
- durability proven, and support in place, before entry into service;
- standard terms only;
- 'no widebody UltraFan until a Re-engine is on the table';
- speak only for Rolls-Royce's own decisions, never for P&W's terms.
Any line on capacity should give no rate or date.

State the objectives plainly in `rationale`:
- narrowbody entry met (25.1% in 2045, 23.3% in 2050);
- widebody dominance met (55.2% at the low point, 58.0% in S4);
- bounded narrowbody risk not met, locked at $5.71B (the Solo's 2030-2035 spend, folded into the Joint Venture);
- widebody never starved met at $0 strain, and kept only by holding both widebody items.
